Here is the step-by-step solution:

We want to compute $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 4 & 8 \\ \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$.  We multiply the first row by the first column of the first matrix and the first element of the second row of the first matrix by the second element of the second column of the first matrix.
\begin{align*} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 4 & 8 \\ \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} (1)(5) + (2)(3) \\ (4)(5) + (8)(3) \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 5 + 6 \\ 20 + 24 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 11 \\ 44 \end{pmatrix} \end{align*}
The answer is $\begin{pmatrix} 11 \\ 44 \end{pmatrix}$.

\boxed{<11, 44>}

