    \item Let $p_1$ be the 1-digit prime number and $p_2$ be the 2-digit prime number such that $p_1 + p_2 = 100$.
    \item We know that prime numbers are numbers greater than 1 that are only divisible by 1 and themselves. The 1-digit prime numbers are $2, 3, 5, 7$.
    \item We have $p_2 = 100 - p_1$. Since $p_2$ is a 2-digit number, $p_2 \geq 10$. Thus, $100 - p_1 \geq 10$, which implies $p_1 \leq 90$. However, we also know that $p_1$ is a 1-digit number, so $p_1 \leq 9$.
    \item Now we can test each possible value of $p_1$.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If $p_1 = 2$, then $p_2 = 100 - 2 = 98$. Since 98 is divisible by 2 (and by 7), it is not a prime number.
        \item If $p_1 = 3$, then $p_2 = 100 - 3 = 97$. Since 97 is a prime number, this is a solution.
        \item If $p_1 = 5$, then $p_2 = 100 - 5 = 95$. Since 95 is divisible by 5, it is not a prime number.
        \item If $p_1 = 7$, then $p_2 = 100 - 7 = 93$. Since 93 is divisible by 3, it is not a prime number.
    \end{itemize}
    \item The only solution is $p_1 = 3$ and $p_2 = 97$.
    \item The product of these prime numbers is $3 \times 97 = 291$.
\end{enumerate}
